<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gediminas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1275–<a href="/years/1341">1341</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Gedimin · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gediminas was Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from about 1316 until his death and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminid dynasty</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> his capital, expanded his realm deep into the lands of Rus' weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and in letters of 1323 invited Western merchants, craftsmen and clergy to settle. Though he stayed pagan, he tolerated Christians, and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestutis">Kęstutis</a> continued his expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gediminas.jpg" alt="Gediminas" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Guagnini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giedzimin._%D0%93%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B7%D1%96%D0%BC%D1%96%D0%BD_(A._Guagnini,_1578).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Traidenis and later Vytenis the state regained strength, and around 1316 Vytenis's brother <b>Gediminas</b>, who gave his name to the Gediminid dynasty, succeeded to a grand duchy that was ready to expand into the lands of Rus' weakened by the Mongol invasions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Trakai, west of Vilnius among lakes, became the seat of Kestutis, son of <b>Gediminas</b>, in the late 1330s.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-trakai">Duchy of Trakai</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia collapsed after 1340, Lubart, a son of <b>Gediminas</b> who had married into the local dynasty, took control of Volhynia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-volhynia">Duchy of Volhynia</a></li><li><blockquote>By the early 14th century Minsk was dependent on Lithuania, and around 1326 a Prince Vasily of Minsk appears as a vassal of <b>Gediminas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-minsk">Principality of Minsk</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mongol invasion its lands weakened further, and in the early 14th century they were absorbed by the Grand Duchy of Lithuania under <b>Gediminas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-turov-and-pinsk">Principality of Turov and Pinsk</a></li><li><blockquote>Algirdas, son of <b>Gediminas</b>, married Maria, daughter of the last prince Yaroslav, and inherited Vitebsk in 1320.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-vitebsk">Principality of Vitebsk</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Veliuona, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.08,23.27&amp;year=1317" aria-label="Veliuona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5060 281 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gediminas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5510 551h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5510 551h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=55.08,23.27&amp;year=1317">What was here in 1317? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
